      "completion_tokens": 573,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in rivers, oceans, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of giant trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Ice Expansion:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, allowing marine life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity:** Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the defining requirement for life as we know it. \n* The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n* It acts as a cushion for organs, regulates internal body temperature through sweating, and acts as the medium for all cellular chemical reactions. \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make the existence of our planet and our bodies possible."
